{
  "result": "I often think about how strange it is that we spend so much of our lives looking down at phones and screens, when just above us there is an entire universe performing quietly every night.\n\nLast summer I lay on a cool blanket in my backyard, watching Perseid meteors streak across the sky. Each flash lasted barely a second, yet it felt like the cosmos was sending tiny postcards, reminding me that we are both insignificant and miraculous at once.\n\nMy neighbor's dog barked somewhere in the dark, crickets hummed in rhythm, and a warm breeze carried the scent of cut grass and distant rain. I realized happiness often hides in these unplanned pauses, not in grand achievements or carefully curated photographs.\n\nWe tell ourselves stories about productivity and success, as if life were a race with a finish line. But maybe it is more like a long walk through unfamiliar woods, where the point is to notice moss on stones, light filtering through leaves, and the way your breath sounds when everything else is silent.\n\nSo lately I have been practicing attention. I savor morning coffee slowly, listen fully when friends speak, and let myself wonder about strangers passing by. What dreams do they carry? What small sorrows or joys shape their days?\n\nIf curiosity is a muscle, then wonder is its reward. The world remains vast, tender, and endlessly surprising for anyone willing to look up again and smile thoughtfully under vast ancient starlight tonight together peacefully always.",
